---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<!-- Master copy — the live file is mmp-site/src/pages/kids.astro. Edit here, copy in, build, push only that file. -->
<Layout title="Kids" description="Kids activities — saved from Instagram and the blog shelf, with instructions you can actually use — plus the conflict-resolution playbook for when kids fight.">
  <main class="bg-[#0c0b09] min-h-screen px-4 py-14 sm:py-20 text-[#ece5d3]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.35em] text-[#c9a86a]">Saved, distilled</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[#f7f0dc]">Kids</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        Activities from the kids collection on Instagram, plus a few from a
        parenting blog, written up with instructions so you don't have to
        re-watch the reel to run them — plus the conflict-resolution stuff,
        so it's all in one place.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">Summaries, not transcripts. Instagram activities link to the original reel; blog ones link to the post. Checked daily; new saves are added here.</p>

      <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Activities</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6">

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Gift guide · Active play</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">High-energy toys worth buying again</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/alexgracelindsay" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@alexgracelindsay</a> — a mum-of-two's repeat-buy list for kids who never stop moving.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">A round-up of the toys this family actually re-buys for active toddlers and preschoolers — things that keep little bodies busy and burn off energy, indoors and out. It's a shopping list, not a single activity: pick two or three that fit your space and rotate them.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">The picks</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Ride-on school bus and electric wiggle car — for zooming around</li>
            <li>Indoor bounce house and outdoor inflatable with slide — the big energy burner</li>
            <li>Giant magnetic tiles — build forts, then knock them down</li>
            <li>Air Toobz ball blower and ball-drop toy — air-powered, endlessly repeatable</li>
            <li>Flexible race track, indoor car slide, and wooden climbing house with swing and rings</li>
            <li>Banwood scooter with helmet, light-up hide-and-seek gem game, and a small basketball hoop</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">How to use it</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Pick one big item (bounce house, climbing house, swing set) and a couple of small ones — don't buy the whole list at once.</li>
            <li>Set the big item up in one clear spot and leave the small toys in a basket the kids can reach themselves.</li>
            <li>Rotate: swap one toy out for another every week or so, so the novelty stays fresh.</li>
            <li>Comment “MOVE” on the original post and the author sends the purchase links.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/p/DfNomoamMqe/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the original post →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Craft · Halloween</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">New crayons from broken crayons</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/nataliia.sahm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@nataliia.sahm</a> — Halloween shapes from the crayon stubs you'd otherwise throw away.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Broken crayons, melted down and remoulded into Halloween shapes — ghosts and pumpkins — that the kids can actually colour with afterwards. A use for the box of stubs, and a decoration in the same move.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Broken crayons, wrappers peeled off</li>
            <li>A silicone mould in Halloween shapes (ghosts, pumpkins)</li>
            <li>An oven</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Peel the wrappers off the broken crayons and snap them into small pieces.</li>
            <li>Fill each mould cavity with crayon pieces — mix colours in one cavity for a marbled effect.</li>
            <li>Melt in a low oven until the wax is fully liquid, then let it cool and set completely.</li>
            <li>Pop the new crayons out of the mould — ready to colour with, or to set out as Halloween decor.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DdCCH-ERXwX/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Sensory · Science</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Lava lamp in a bin</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/simple.fun.play" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@simple.fun.play</a> — three ingredients, and it holds kids of any age for a long time.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Coloured milk dropped into oil sinks in slow, wobbly blobs — a lava lamp you make in a storage bin. Messy in a good way, and genuinely mesmerising to watch.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>About a cup of milk</li>
            <li>Food dye in a few colours</li>
            <li>Vegetable oil (any oil that doesn't solidify — not coconut oil)</li>
            <li>A bin or shallow tray, and a dropper or syringe</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Divide the milk between small bowls and dye each one a different colour.</li>
            <li>Cover the bottom of the bin with oil.</li>
            <li>Use the dropper or syringe to drop the coloured milk into the oil, a few drops at a time.</li>
            <li>Watch the milk sink and swirl through the oil — add more drops and more colours as it goes.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> milk is denser than oil, so the drops sink through it slowly instead of mixing — that's the lava-lamp effect.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DezhrguJGnU/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Screen-free · Toddlers</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Floating spoon drawing</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/mom.in.a.million" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@mom.in.a.million</a> — day 8 of a 30-day screen-free activities series.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">A drawing that lifts off a spoon and floats on water. It genuinely stops toddlers in their tracks — and needs almost nothing.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>A metal spoon</li>
            <li>A dry-erase marker</li>
            <li>Cold water in a bowl or tray</li>
            <li>Paper, if you want to lift the print off</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Draw on the back of the spoon with the dry-erase marker.</li>
            <li>Slowly dip the spoon into the cold water.</li>
            <li>Watch the drawing float off the spoon onto the water's surface.</li>
            <li>Lay a piece of paper on top to transfer the print, like a tattoo.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> dry-erase ink doesn't bond to metal the way permanent ink does, so water getting underneath lifts it right off.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeuTsiGJ3qM/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Indoor · Burn energy</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Top 5 indoor energy-burning activities</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/craftmonsterz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@craftmonsterz</a> — counted down from 5 to 1.</p>
          <ol class="mt-5 space-y-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]" reversed>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">5. Tape Jump:</strong> lay down play cushions and stick masking tape on the wall. Jump between two pieces of tape, or over one piece — then move the tape higher after every round and see how high you can go before you break it.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">4. Treasure Rescue:</strong> put a pool noodle down and a bunch of stuffed animals behind it. One adult guards the “treasure” while the kids sneak or run around to grab a stuffed animal.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">3. Stuffie Knockdown:</strong> simple aim and coordination practice — knock the stuffed animals down, with giggles built in.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">2. Clear the Room:</strong> put a long piece of cardboard in the middle of the room with a hole cut in the middle. Set a timer for one minute; each team throws balls through the hole to clear their side. No throwing over the box. Fewest balls left on your side wins.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">1. Hula Hoop Jump:</strong> the all-time favourite in this house — dead simple, and it keeps kids entertained and burning energy the longest.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DWyzOVmCdAw/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the original reel →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Screen-free · Sensory</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Oat sensory bin</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Little Nest</a> — screen-free activities for cold days.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Dry oats in a tray, cups and spoons for scooping and pouring. Quiet, absorbing, and safe if Tzofia mouths a little.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Dry rolled oats</li>
            <li>A tray, baking dish, or shallow tub</li>
            <li>Cups, spoons, and small containers</li>
            <li>A few small toys to bury and dig up, if you like</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Pour a few centimetres of dry oats into the tray.</li>
            <li>Hand over the cups and spoons and let them scoop, pour, and fill.</li>
            <li>Bury a small toy in the oats and let them dig it out.</li>
            <li>Put a sheet or old towel underneath — oats travel.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> scooping and pouring are the whole game at this age — fine-motor practice that feels like play, and dry oats are harmless if a two-year-old tastes them.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original post →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Indoor · Burn energy</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Cushion obstacle course</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Little Nest</a> — screen-free activities for cold days.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Pillows to climb over, a blanket tunnel to crawl through, a laundry basket at the end. Burns energy with zero supplies to buy.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Cushions and pillows</li>
            <li>A blanket, draped over chairs for a tunnel</li>
            <li>A laundry basket to crawl through or climb into</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Lay the pillows in a row to climb over.</li>
            <li>Drape the blanket over two chairs to make a tunnel.</li>
            <li>Put the laundry basket at the end as the finish — crawl through it or climb in.</li>
            <li>Time a run, then let them rearrange the course and run it again.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> climbing, crawling, and squeezing through gaps is exactly the heavy work little bodies need indoors — and rebuilding the course is half the fun. Keep the piles low for Tzofia.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original post →</a></p>
        </article>

        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-[#c9a86a]">Screen-free · Freezer prep</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">Ice excavation</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">From <a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Little Nest</a> — screen-free activities for cold days.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">Small toy animals frozen in a block of ice, rescued with warm water. Needs an overnight freeze — prep it the night before, not the morning of.</p>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">You need</h4>
          <ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>Small toy animals</li>
            <li>A container of water, big enough to hold them</li>
            <li>A freezer, and an overnight wait</li>
            <li>Warm (not hot) water in a squeeze bottle or jug</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="mt-5 font-bold text-[#ece5d3]">Instructions</h4>
          <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li>The night before: put the toys in the container, cover with water, and freeze overnight.</li>
            <li>Turn the ice block out into a tray or tub.</li>
            <li>Hand over the squeeze bottle of warm water and let them melt the animals free.</li>
            <li>Keep a towel handy — this one gets wet.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]"><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Why it works:</strong> the rescue mission gives the melting a purpose, and warm water in their own hands makes the cause-and-effect obvious. Prep it tomorrow night for next Shabbat.</p>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="https://thelittlenestblog.netlify.app/posts/screen-free-activities-cold-days/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original post →</a></p>
        </article>

      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">When kids fight: conflict resolution</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad] max-w-2xl">
        The short version of the sibling-fighting page, kept here so the kids
        page is self-contained. The full page has the scripts, the disagreements,
        and the red flags.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6">
        <article class="rounded-2xl border border-[#c9a86a]/30 bg-white/5 p-7 sm:p-8">
          <h3 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-[#f7f0dc]">The 60-second playbook</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 leading-relaxed text-[#c9c0ad]">
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Pause first:</strong> stop touching, move apart, breathe, check in — then resume. Start at 15 seconds, build to 30, then a minute. It's a scaffold for self-regulation, not a punishment.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Coach, don't referee:</strong> don't take sides or hand down a verdict. Narrate what you see, validate it, then hand the problem back: “What are you guys going to do about it?” Conflict is where kids learn to disagree, share power, and repair.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Toddlers can't self-resolve:</strong> stop the hurt first, help both kids regulate, and only then teach the missing skill once calm — e.g. “You wanted the toy. Try: ‘Can I have a turn?’”</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Fair ≠ equal:</strong> “We don't do fair. We do individual needs.” A protest is often really “Do I matter as much?” — name what the other child needed, hold the boundary, and let disappointment exist.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-[#ece5d3]">Step in for safety, not verdicts:</strong> separate and create space when bodies or feelings are unsafe. The full page covers where rivalry ends and bullying begins.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-5"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="/parenting/sibling-fighting">Full sibling-fighting page, with scripts and sources →</a></p>
          <p class="mt-2"><a class="text-[#c9a86a] hover:underline" href="/parenting">All parenting topics →</a></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-14 text-sm text-[#8a8474]">Sources: @matthewjmiller07 Instagram saved collection “kids” (5 items) plus The Little Nest blog (3 ideas), last checked 11 Nov 2026 (8 items total). This page is a personal summary project, not medical or psychological advice.</p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
